<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santa Sofia, Benevento</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 760</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Santa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> is a church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, founded around 760 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> duke Arechis II as the national shrine of the southern Lombards. Its unusual star-shaped plan, with a central hexagon of ancient columns, makes it one of the most original surviving early medieval buildings. Since 2011 it has been part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage site <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombards">Longobards</a> in Italy, Places of Power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santa-sofia-benevento.jpg" alt="Santa Sofia, Benevento" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santa_Sofia,_Benevento_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arechis-ii-of-benevento">Arechis II of Benevento</a> founded the church around 760, as numerous privileges he signed attest; some of them are kept in the Museum of Samnium beside it. It was modelled on the palatine chapel of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king Liutprand at Pavia. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a> and the Lombard kingdom of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> fell in 774, it became the national church of the Lombards who had taken refuge in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>. It belonged to a wider building programme meant to support Arechis's claim to be the highest Lombard authority after his failed attempt to take the title of king.</p><p>Arechis dedicated the church to Holy Wisdom, like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and attached to it a Benedictine convent, formerly dependent on Montecassino, under his sister Gariperga. It also housed the relics of Saint Mercurius.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The church is built on a central plan 23.5 metres across. Six columns, perhaps taken from the city's ancient Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>, stand at the corners of a hexagon and carry the dome on arches; around them runs a ten-sided ring of eight white limestone pillars and two columns beside the entrance. The area of the three apses is circular, while the front forms part of a star with niches in its corners. The raised centre recalls the lost church of Santa Maria in Pertica at Pavia, and the grouping of volumes shows Byzantine influence. Fragments of frescoes of the late 8th or early 9th century, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annunciation">Annunciation</a> and the Visitation, survive in the side apses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Earthquakes in 1688 and 1702 brought down the original dome, and Cardinal Orsini, the future Pope Benedict XIII, had the church rebuilt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style from 1705, with a circular plan. Most of its original form was restored in 1957, though the Baroque façade remains, with a Romanesque portal and a 13th-century relief. Its 12th-century cloister leads to the Samnium Museum. In 2011 it became part of the UNESCO World Heritage Site Longobards in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Places of Power.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Santa Sofia, Benevento” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Charlemagne took the north in 774, Arechis II took the title of prince and claimed the Lombard heritage, building the church of <b>Santa Sofia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-benevento">Duchy of Benevento</a></li></ul></section>
